 ### [Smoothed 2D Supervision](7213-smoothed-2d-supervision.md)
-Smoothed 2D Supervision stabilizes the joint optimization of camera poses and 3D radiance fields by blurring 2D training images with scheduled 2D Gaussian kernels. It produces smoothed image gradients to prevent noisy gradient updates and restricts the high-frequency spectrum of the learned scene. Unlike [Frequency Regularization](6130-frequency-regularization.md) which operates in the frequency domain, this method applies spatial-domain smoothing directly to the target images before loss computation.
+Smoothed 2D Supervision is a regularization technique that blurs 2D training images with a scheduled set of 2D Gaussian kernels to produce smoothed image gradients, stabilize camera alignment, and restrict the spectrum of the learned 3D scene. It sets itself apart from fixed-size smoothing by progressively reducing the blur intensity, allowing the model to transition from low-frequency stability to high-frequency detail. It differs from [Frequency Regularization](6130-frequency-regularization.md) by operating directly on the pixel supervision target rather than adjusting positional encoding frequencies, and from [Depth Regularization](7616-depth-regularization.md) by focusing on 2D image-space consistency rather than geometric depth constraints. See [Smoothed 2D Supervision](7213-smoothed-2d-supervision.md).
 
 ### [Depth Regularization](7616-depth-regularization.md)
-Depth regularization methods enforce geometric plausibility in differentiable volume rendering by minimizing the discrepancy between rendered depth maps and external depth targets (monocular estimates, stereo matches, or LiDAR). This family addresses the inherent ambiguity of single-view or sparse-view reconstruction, where color-only supervision often leads to incorrect Gaussian positioning, floating artifacts, or collapsed geometry. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Frequency Regularization](6130-frequency-regularization.md) or [Smoothed 2D Supervision](7213-smoothed-2d-supervision.md) by operating directly on the geometric depth channel rather than color space or frequency domains, providing explicit metric or relative depth constraints.
