 ### [Hard and Soft Depth Regularization](9178-hard-and-soft-depth-regularization.md)
-Hard and Soft Depth Regularization is a technique for constraining the geometry of 3D Gaussian Splatting by encouraging spatial reshaping (movement of centers) rather than deforming Gaussian shapes, thereby preventing detail loss and blurred appearance caused by forcing anisotropic Gaussians to fit smooth monocular depth. It sets itself apart from standard depth regularization methods by decoupling the optimization of center positions and opacities through two distinct rendered depth maps, while freezing shape parameters (scale and rotation) during the process. This approach addresses the conflict where NeRF-style depth regularization forces primitive geometry to conform to smooth estimates, which conflicts with complex color appearance.
