 ## Background
-The need for regularization in 3D Gaussian Splatting arises from the fact that standard optimization relies primarily on photometric loss ($\mathcal{L}_{rgb}$), which can lead to unstructured density clouds that do not represent valid surfaces [S3]. To recover high-quality geometry, recent methods impose physical constraints during optimization, such as integrating Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) [S3] or using monocular depth priors [S3]. Additionally, to prevent overfitting and high-frequency artifacts, techniques like FreGS apply progressive frequency regularization [S4, unofficial]. Other issues, such as oscillating gradients in scaling and rotation parameters, are addressed by rescaling gradients [S4, unofficial]. The integration of depth guidance has also been explored in related neural radiance field frameworks, such as DINER, which uses depth-aware sampling and source feature extrapolation to improve synthesis quality [S2].
+The original 3D Gaussian Splatting method optimizes for photometric loss, which often results in unstructured density clouds that do not represent a valid surface [S3]. To recover high-quality geometry, recent methods impose physical constraints during optimization [S3]. This includes integrating Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) into the 3DGS framework [S3], using monocular depth priors [S3], or applying frequency regularization to ensure consistent appearance across resolutions [S4, unofficial]. Popping artifacts arising from depth-sorting steps have also been addressed by replacing global sorting with per-pixel depth comparisons or integrating ray-tracing kernels [S3]. Additionally, gradient oscillations in scaling and rotation parameters have been stabilized by rescaling gradients with the inverse of the current scale [S4, unofficial].
 
